Wokwi has no TCRT5000 part, so simulate **what the Arduino sees**, which is what matters for the code:

- A **potentiometer** on A0 stands in for the analog output (turn it up = darker surface).
- A **slide switch** to GND on D2 with `INPUT_PULLUP` stands in for DO (LOW = reflection detected).
- An LED on D7 (220 Ω).

```arduino
const int AOUT = A0, DOUT = 2, LED = 7;
const int BLACK_THRESHOLD = 600;   // tune on the real module

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(DOUT, INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(LED, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  int raw = analogRead(AOUT);
  bool onLine = raw > BLACK_THRESHOLD;          // high value = little reflection = black
  bool reflect = digitalRead(DOUT) == LOW;      // the module's own comparator
  digitalWrite(LED, onLine);
  Serial.print("raw:"); Serial.print(raw);
  Serial.print(",line:"); Serial.print(onLine * 1000);
  Serial.print(",reflect:"); Serial.println(reflect * 1000);
  delay(50);
}
```

Sweep the pot slowly through 600 and watch the LED flicker at the boundary. That flicker is the reason for hysteresis on Thursday. Write down in one sentence why a value hovering at the threshold toggles.

Nothing to guess here: read the example and its result. The Learn step explains why it behaves this way.

```cpp-snippet
class Tank {
public:
    double level() { return level_; }
    void fill(double l) { level_ = l; }
private:
    double level_ = 0;
};

void report(const Tank& t) {
    double x = t.level();      // (1)
}

int main() {
    Tank t;
    t.fill(3.5);               // (2)
    t.level_ = 9;              // (3)
    const Tank empty;
    empty.fill(1);             // (4)
}
```

**Which lines compile:** (1) fails, (2) compiles, (3) fails, (4) fails.

- (1) fails because `level()` isn't marked `const`, so it can't be called on a `const Tank&`.
- (3) fails because `level_` is private.
- (4) fails because `empty` is `const` and `fill` changes it.

The first one is today's main lesson.

## Public, private and the invariant

`public` members are the class's interface: what other code may call. `private` members are the implementation: only the class's own member functions (and friends) may touch them. The compiler enforces this, which is the whole point. In the first example, `t.level_ = 9;` fails because `level_` is private.

Why bother? Because a class with private data has **one place** where its rules live. Look at the `MovingAverage` below: it keeps a running sum so `average()` is O(1). That only works if `sum_` always equals the sum of the values in the buffer. If `sum_` were public, any caller could break that, and the bug would show up far from its cause. Private data plus member functions that maintain the invariant means you only have to check the class to trust it.

A practical rule: **make data private by default**; expose behaviour (`add`, `withdraw`, `set`) rather than setters that let callers write any value. A getter is fine; a setter that just assigns is often a sign the rule belongs inside the class.

## const member functions

Every non-static member function receives a hidden pointer `this` to the object it was called on. Writing `const` after the parameter list makes `this` a pointer to const: the function promises not to modify the object, and the compiler checks the promise.

The other side of that rule: **on a const object, or through a const reference, you can only call const member functions.** That's why (1) failed: `report` receives a `const Tank&`, and `level()` wasn't marked `const`, so the compiler must assume it modifies the tank. Since week 9 you pass objects by `const&` everywhere, so any getter that isn't marked `const` becomes unusable. Rule: **every member function that doesn't modify the object gets `const`.** (4) fails for the same reason in the other direction: `fill` modifies, so it can't be called on a const object.

Getters that return a big member return it by `const&` (`const std::string& name() const`), so callers can read without copying and can't modify through it.

## Defining members outside the class

For anything longer than a line, declare in the class and define outside with the scope operator `ClassName::`:

```cpp-snippet
// moving_average.h
#pragma once
#include <cstddef>
#include <vector>

class MovingAverage {
public:
    explicit MovingAverage(std::size_t window);
    void add(double x);
    double average() const;
private:
    std::vector<double> buf_;
    std::size_t next_ = 0, count_ = 0;
    double sum_ = 0.0;
};

// moving_average.cpp
#include "moving_average.h"
MovingAverage::MovingAverage(std::size_t window) : buf_(window == 0 ? 1 : window, 0.0) {}
double MovingAverage::average() const { return count_ == 0 ? 0.0 : sum_ / count_; }
```

Note that `const` is repeated in the definition: it's part of the function's signature. Functions defined inside the class body are implicitly `inline`, which is why they can live in a header without breaking the one-definition rule.

## Worked example: a moving average for noisy sensors

This is exactly what you'll need in tomorrow's robotics prep to smooth an ultrasonic sensor. A ring buffer of the last `n` readings and a running sum:

```cpp
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

class MovingAverage {
public:
    explicit MovingAverage(std::size_t window) : buf_(window == 0 ? 1 : window, 0.0) {}
    void add(double x);
    double average() const { return count_ == 0 ? 0.0 : sum_ / count_; }
    std::size_t count() const { return count_; }
private:
    std::vector<double> buf_;
    std::size_t next_ = 0;   // where the next value goes
    std::size_t count_ = 0;  // how many slots hold real values
    double sum_ = 0.0;       // invariant: sum of the real values in buf_
};

void MovingAverage::add(double x) {
    if (count_ == buf_.size()) sum_ -= buf_[next_];  // full: drop the oldest
    else ++count_;
    buf_[next_] = x;
    sum_ += x;
    next_ = (next_ + 1) % buf_.size();
}

int main() {
    MovingAverage m{3};
    for (double x : {10.0, 20.0, 30.0, 40.0}) {
        m.add(x);
        std::cout << m.average() << ' ';   // 10 15 20 30
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
}
```

Trace the fourth call: the buffer is full (`count_ == 3`), `next_` is 0 and holds 10, so the sum goes 60 - 10 + 40 = 90 and the average is 30. The invariant holds after every `add`, and no outside code can touch `sum_`.

